Subject: winex11, server: report a touchpad pinch as Ctrl-tagged wheel input

Wine reports touchpad input as pointer and wheel events only. A pinch
reaches the X server as an XInput2 gesture. winex11 never selects
gesture events, so the X server drops the pinch and Live has no zoom on
a trackpad other than Ctrl+scroll (issue 64).

Select XI_GesturePinchBegin, XI_GesturePinchUpdate and XI_GesturePinchEnd
on each top-level window, next to the touch events winex11 already
selects there, and report the scale change as wheel input that
applications read as Ctrl+wheel. This is the input a Windows precision
touchpad produces for a pinch its application does not handle itself, so
applications that zoom on Ctrl+wheel need no change. PinchZoom=disabled
leaves gesture events with the X server and keeps the XInput2 version
request at 2.2.

The patch synthesizes no Ctrl key event anywhere: no key message exists,
no keyboard hook sees a phantom key, and no accelerator can fire. The
modifier reaches the application through both of its observable reads:

- A new SEND_HWMSG_FORCE_MK_CONTROL flag on the send_hardware_message
  request makes the server set MK_CONTROL in the generated message's
  wparam, which it builds at queue time. update_key_state() ignores the
  MK_* bits of mouse messages, so this half changes no key state. The
  request's wire format does not change and the client passes its
  existing flags word through, so the protocol needs no version bump; a
  later patch in the series already carries the next bump.
- For the gesture's duration, the driver holds Ctrl in the owning
  thread's queue key state and the async key state through the server's
  existing set_key_state request. Live reads GetKeyState() rather than
  the wheel message state. A run against Live 12 on 2026-08-10 verified
  this: with the wparam override alone, the wheel arrived and nothing
  zoomed. This half serves that read without creating any input. The
  driver releases the key on gesture end, cancellation, focus loss,
  device change, slot eviction and thread detach. It checks the X keymap for a Ctrl the
  user physically holds and never releases that key. And if every
  release path misses, a stale state byte self-heals through the
  KeymapNotify modifier resync.

A whole wheel notch stands for a 1.1x scale change, but a pinch reports
its scale many times a second, so each report is worth a fraction of one
notch. The driver sends that fraction: WHEEL_DELTA is a unit, not a
minimum, and an application that follows the amount rather than counting
messages zooms continuously instead of in steps. Each report advances
the tracked scale by exactly what the driver sent, so the remainder
carries into the next report. The driver caps a single report at 16
notches.

The driver tracks gestures per physical device: the event's sourceid
keys the slots, not the master deviceid, so two slave devices pinching
through one master pointer stay two gestures. XInput2 allows one active
gesture sequence per device. A begin seeds the baseline at the
protocol's defined begin scale of 1.0. An update without a begin claims
a slot and seeds from its own scale. The driver ignores an end without a
begin.

The driver undoes a cancelled gesture (XIGesturePinchEventCancelled) the
way the protocol asks: it returns the net wheel movement the gesture
reported in one compensating event. When the application clamped the
zoom at a bound, the compensation can overshoot. For a transient
misrecognized gesture that is the lesser wrong against leaving a phantom
zoom applied.

The wheel events carry no position: the server stamps them at the
current cursor, which a touchpad pinch does not move. The path keeps no
frozen anchor and makes no XQueryPointer round trip.

The server's wheel coalescing gains two guards this work depends on. The
server never merges across a modifier change: summing an untagged scroll
delta into a Ctrl-tagged zoom message would change what the combined
delta means. And it coalesces WM_MOUSEHWHEEL the same way it has always
coalesced WM_MOUSEWHEEL, so a stalled application drains one accumulated
message per axis instead of hundreds of queued horizontal events.

Gesture events need XInput2 2.4. The server refuses to select them for a
client that asked for less, so the version request rises to 2.4 only
when PinchZoom is on. On an X server without 2.4 the driver warns once
and behaves as before; headers without the gesture definitions compile
the path out.

A verification run on GNOME Wayland (XWayland) on 2026-08-10 showed
pinch zoom working in Live 12 with this design.
